    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The turn from the tenth to the eleventh century was a precipice moment for Byzantine women, laden with the surviving vestiges of iconoclast social ideals and a burst of women – imperial, common, and religious – entering the public sphere. Hagiographer and bureaucrat Symeon Metaphrastes's redactions of Pelagia of Antioch's and Barbara the Martyr's vitae display a concerted effort to maintain Late Antique social norms for women well into the tenth century that promoted keeping them segregated from men. As a source unique in its breadth of access, Symeon Metaphrastes was perhaps not representative of trends within the whole genre of hagiography. The vitae of two women sanctified contemporary to Symeon’s project, however, underscore strikingly similar themes. The Lives of Thomaïs of Lesbos and Mary the Younger use similar language to promote marriage and the social unit of the family – one that encouraged women to stay at home. Both Barbara and Pelagia are androgenized in a manner still appealing to a male audience’s sexual appetite for women. Thomaïs’s and Mary’s narratives necessitated an emphasis on their gender roles within their families that brought their social roles as women to the forefront of their texts. The establishment institutions of both the Church and state in Byzantium used the increasingly political medium of hagiography as an assault against the growing physical presence of women in Byzantine society disguised as theological altruism. Ultimately, these women’s vitae highlight an increased fixation with gender in religious writings at the outset of the eleventh century that contributed to the steady decline, then eventual disappearance of new narratives of female sanctity in Byzantium over the course of the following centuries.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
